<% content_for :title, @notion_connection.name %>

<div class="stack container--wide">
  <div class="cluster" style="justify-content: space-between;">
    <div>
      <p class="text-muted"><%= link_to "← Back to Notion connections", admin_notion_connections_path %></p>
      <h1><%= @notion_connection.name %></h1>
      <p class="text-muted">
        Syncs into <strong><%= @notion_connection.area.path_name %></strong> —
        <% if @notion_connection.active? %>
          <span class="badge badge--success">Active</span>
        <% else %>
          <span class="badge">Disabled</span>
        <% end %>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="cluster">
      <%= link_to "Edit", edit_admin_notion_connection_path(@notion_connection), class: "btn btn--outlined" %>
      <%= button_to "Delete", admin_notion_connection_path(@notion_connection), method: :delete,
            data: { turbo_confirm: "Delete \"#{@notion_connection.name}\"? Notion will keep sending events here, but Atlas will reject them." },
            class: "btn btn--danger" %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="docs-section stack">
    <h2>Webhook URL</h2>
    <p>Paste this into Notion's connection page — <strong>Webhooks tab → Create a subscription</strong>.</p>
    <pre class="code-block" style="word-break: break-all; white-space: pre-wrap;"><%= @webhook_url %></pre>
    <p class="field__hint">
      Keep this URL as private as a password until Notion's own signature verification is active below —
      until then, it's the only thing standing in for authentication.
    </p>
    <%= button_to "Regenerate webhook URL", regenerate_webhook_token_admin_notion_connection_path(@notion_connection), method: :post,
          data: { turbo_confirm: "Generate a new webhook URL for \"#{@notion_connection.name}\"? Update it in Notion's Webhooks tab or events will stop arriving." },
          class: "btn btn--outlined btn--sm" %>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section stack">
    <% if @notion_connection.awaiting_verification? %>
      <h2>Verification — waiting</h2>
      <div class="card card--outlined">
        <p>
          Notion hasn't contacted this URL yet. In Notion, paste the webhook URL above into
          <strong>Create a subscription</strong>, choose which events to send (<code class="font-mono">Page content updated</code>,
          <code class="font-mono">Page created</code>, and <code class="font-mono">Page properties updated</code> are the ones
          Atlas actually acts on — others are safe to leave unchecked), and save. Reload this page afterward —
          Notion's one-time handshake request is what fills in the token below.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% else %>
      <h2>Verification token</h2>
      <p>
        Notion sent this the moment the subscription was created above. Copy it into Notion's own
        <strong>⚠️ Verify → Verify subscription</strong> prompt to finish activating it there — until you do,
        Notion won't send any real events yet.
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block" style="word-break: break-all; white-space: pre-wrap;"><%= @notion_connection.verification_token %></pre>
      <p class="field__hint">
        From here on, this same value is also what Atlas checks every real event against
        (<code class="font-mono">X-Notion-Signature</code>) — it isn't just for that one-time paste.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section stack">
    <h2>What happens on sync</h2>
    <ul class="stack" style="gap: 8px;">
      <li>A page created or edited in Notion (and shared with this connection there) creates or updates a matching Atlas page in <strong><%= @notion_connection.area.path_name %></strong> — matched by Notion's own page ID, so repeated edits update the same Atlas page rather than creating duplicates.</li>
      <li>New pages are always created <strong>private</strong>, regardless of the area's own visibility — someone still reviews before making it public, same reasoning as the Basecamp integration landing as a draft.</li>
      <li>Deleting a page in Notion does <strong>not</strong> delete or hide it here — Atlas only reacts to content changes, never removals, so nothing already reviewed here disappears out from under someone.</li>
      <li>Not everything Notion can contain converts — child pages, databases, embeds, and a handful of other block types are skipped rather than guessed at; text, headings, lists, quotes, code, images, simple tables, and to-dos all carry over.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
